

:: day_1_1
<<set $day_step to 1>>
The crack in the ceiling looks like a river delta.

I've been staring at it long enough to name the tributaries. The main fissure runs diagonally from somewhere above the kitchenette to a point directly over my pillow   a slow-motion fault line in cheap plaster that's been widening since I moved in eight months ago. There are smaller branches splitting off from it, hairline fractures that spider outward like the whole ceiling is a windshield that took a rock on the highway. Sometimes, in that grey zone between sleep and waking, I imagine I can hear it growing. A tiny, geological creak. The building settling further into its own decay.

My phone alarm is going off.

It's been going off for a while, actually. The sound has woven itself into whatever half-dream I was having   something about being in a lobby that kept loading, a progress bar stuck at 99%, and a chat window scrolling too fast to read. The alarm is a default tone I never bothered to change, a cheerful marimba loop that feels like mockery at   I fumble the phone off the floor beside the mattress and squint at the screen   11:47 AM.

I set it for 10:30.

The screen brightness stabs my eyes and I drop the phone face-down on my chest, blinking at the ceiling crack while my pupils adjust. My mouth tastes like the inside of a USB port. There's a dull ache behind my left eye that might be dehydration or might be the Monster Energy I drank at 3 AM finally metabolizing into regret. Both, probably.

A Discord notification sits under the alarm. From last night. I already read it before I fell asleep   read it twice, actually, like an idiot   but my eyes find it again before I can stop them.

<<kira "night eli! don't stay up too late okay? you need your beauty sleep 😴✨">>

Beauty sleep. She said beauty sleep. With a sparkle emoji. I know it's a joke. I know she says stuff like that to everyone. Kira's the kind of person who calls people "babe" and "hon" the way other people say "dude"   it doesn't mean anything, it's just her frequency, her warmth spilling out in all directions without a specific target. I know this. I've had this conversation with myself approximately four hundred times.

I still read the message a third time before I put the phone down. I should get up.

The mattress   a queen-size I found on the sidewalk three blocks over, dragged home at midnight with Marcus's help, and swore I'd buy a frame for   is on the floor against the wall beneath the window. The sheets are grey. They were white when I bought them from the dollar store but they've been through enough wash cycles in the basement machines to achieve a permanent, institutional grey that no amount of detergent will fix. The fitted sheet has popped off the far corner again and bunched up, exposing a yellowed stain on the mattress that was there when I found it and which I've chosen to believe is coffee.

My apartment is a studio. That word   studio   does a lot of heavy lifting. It implies intention, like someone designed this 280-square-foot rectangle to be a living space rather than a storage unit that someone illegally subdivided and rented to a desperate 22-year-old for $875 a month. The kitchenette is against the far wall: a mini-fridge that hums at a frequency I can feel in my teeth, a hot plate, a microwave with a broken turntable so you have to open it and rotate your food manually halfway through. The bathroom is behind a door that doesn't fully close because the humidity warped the frame. There's a closet, but the rod fell down four months ago and I just pile clothes on the floor in there now and then there's the desk.

The desk is the point of this whole arrangement. Everything else   the mattress, the kitchenette, the oxygen   exists in service to the desk. It's a battered wooden thing I pulled off the curb on bulk trash day, slightly warped on one side so my mouse pad slopes if I don't wedge a folded piece of cardboard under the left leg. On it: two monitors. The main one is a 24-inch I got refurbished for eighty bucks   decent color, responsive enough for gaming, a scratch across the lower left corner that I've trained my eyes to ignore. The second is a 19-inch piece of shit with a cluster of dead pixels in the upper right that looks like a tiny permanent bruise on reality. I use it for chat and stream management. Between them, a budget USB microphone on a short desk stand that I have to lean toward when I talk, which means I spend entire streams slightly hunched, which is doing great things for my posture. The webcam is clipped to the top of the main monitor. It's old enough to be in a museum. In low light   which is always, because my apartment has the ambient brightness of a crypt   it makes my face look like it was rendered on a PlayStation 2.

No ring light. No acoustic panels. No branded overlay. No custom emotes. No sub badges. No alerts. Just a desk, two screens, a mic, a camera from the Obama administration, and the stubborn, stupid refusal to quit that I keep mistaking for ambition.

I've seen Kira's setup in the background of her selfies. Pink-and-black custom keyboard. Dual monitors with clean bezels. Acoustic foam covered in anime posters. A ring light that makes her skin glow like she's being rendered in real time. Everything deliberate, everything polished, everything her   and she built it from scratch, same as me, except hers actually worked.

I pull myself upright. The sheets fall away. I'm wearing the same boxers and grey t-shirt I fell asleep in. My feet find the cold floor   actual hardwood, the one accidental luxury of this apartment, except it's January and there's a draft from somewhere and the wood feels like it's been stored in a morgue.

I shuffle to the bathroom. Don't look in the mirror. I've gotten good at this   navigating the sink, the toilet, the shower, all without making eye contact with the glass. It's not that I'm horrified by what I see. It's worse than that. It's that what I see is nothing. Forgettable. A face that exists in the space between faces, the kind you'd scroll past in a lineup without your brain even registering it as a person. Sometimes I catch myself in a reflection   a shop window, a dark phone screen   and there's this half-second where I genuinely don't recognize myself. Not in a dramatic, dissociative way. Just in the way you don't notice a beige wall.

Kira posted a selfie in the Discord last week. New earrings   little dangling stars, silver, catching the light from her ring light. She had that look she does, the half-smirk with one eyebrow slightly raised, like she knows something you don't and might tell you if you ask nicely. Her bob was freshly cut, the bangs sitting perfectly straight across her forehead. She captioned it "new earrings who dis" and I spent eleven minutes trying to come up with a reply that was funny without being weird, flirty without being desperate, casual without being indifferent. I settled on a thumbs up emoji. A thumbs up. Like a forty-year-old dad reacting to a family group chat. She hearted it, which either meant she found it endearing or she was being polite to a sad person, and I'll never know which.

Shower. Lukewarm because the hot water takes eight minutes to arrive and I'm not waiting. I wash my hair with the same bar soap I use on my body because shampoo is a luxury I keep forgetting to prioritize. The water pressure is a joke   a thin, dispirited stream that couldn't rinse a thought, let alone the grease out of my hair. I get out, towel off with the one towel I own   formerly navy, now a streaky grey-blue that matches the sheets in their shared journey toward entropy.

Clothes. The rotation is three t-shirts: black (least stained), grey (softest), dark green with some graphic that's cracked and peeled beyond recognition (most comfortable). Today is a black t-shirt day. Same joggers as yesterday. Same joggers as the day before. They're black, which means they hide everything, which means they're clean until proven otherwise.

I catch myself, for one stupid second, wondering what Kira would think of this outfit. Then I remember that "outfit" implies a decision was made, and what I'm wearing is less a choice than a default state, and that Kira   who shows up to casual hangouts in band tees and platform sneakers like she just stepped off the set of a music video about being effortlessly cool   would probably not have a thought about my joggers one way or the other.

Stop thinking about her. Stream first. Self-loathing later.

Breakfast is an act of optimism performed with the materials of despair. I open the mini-fridge: half a bottle of generic-brand yellow mustard, a sleeve of American cheese slices with the edges curling, two Monster Energy cans, and something in a Tupperware container that I can't identify and don't open. The freezer has a stack of microwave burritos. I take one, nuke it for two minutes (opening the microwave at one minute to manually rotate it because the turntable's broken, like I said), and eat it standing at the counter while it's still molten in the center and frozen at one end. This is fine. This is fuel. I wash it down with a Monster   the green one, original flavor, the only consistent thing in my life.

11:58 AM. Time to stream.
<<set $hint = "Start the PC and Stream.">>
[[Start the PC|apartment_map]]


:: day_1_2
<<nobr>>
<<set $hint to "Start streaming.">>
<<set $viewers to 3>>
<<set $day_step to 2>>
<</nobr>>
I sit at the desk. The chair is a black mesh office chair I got from a closing sale at an office supply store   $35, one armrest slightly loose, a hydraulic cylinder that slowly sinks over the course of a session so I start each stream at normal height and end it three inches shorter. I pull it in. The desk wobbles. I adjust the cardboard wedge under the left leg with my foot. Open the main monitor: wallpaper is the default Windows 11 bloom that I never changed. Desktop cluttered with game shortcuts, unsorted screenshots, and a folder called "Stream Assets" that contains exactly two files   a PNG of my stream name in a free font and a JPEG of a "be right back" screen I made in Paint.

I open OBS. The interface is a grid of anxiety. My scene is bare-bones: a game capture, a webcam overlay in the bottom left corner   small, because the webcam quality is so bad that the bigger I make it, the more it looks like I'm streaming through a potato   and a text overlay with my stream name: EliPlays. No animations. No transitions. I tried to set up a follower alert once but gave up after an hour of debugging.

I open the game. Gunmetal Requiem   competitive tactical shooter, biggest game on Twitch right now. I'm not good at it. I'm not bad enough to be funny and not good enough to be impressive. I exist in the vast, unmarketable middle of the skill bell curve where nobody wants to watch you because there's nothing to see. I queue into a match while I pull up my stream dashboard on the second monitor.

EliPlays   Live
Viewers: 3

Three. On a good day I peak at maybe 14 or 15. On a bad day, it's the bots and xX_DarkSlayer_Xx, who I'm increasingly convinced is a real person whose sole entertainment is watching small streamers suffer.

I hit "Go Live." The webcam activates. I see myself in the preview   a small, grainy rectangle of a pale face with dark circles, bad hair, and the haunted expression of someone who's about to pretend to be having fun for an audience that doesn't exist.

<<eli "Hey, what's up guys, welcome to the stream. Uh, playing some Gunmetal today. Ranked grind. Let's see if we can climb out of Silver.">>

The chat is a wasteland. Three viewers. No messages. The counter sits there, a little number in the corner of my dashboard, like a test score I already know I failed.

I play. The first match is a disaster   I peek a corner I shouldn't peek and get headshot by someone with a reaction time that suggests they might actually be a targeting algorithm rather than a human being. I respawn, try to play smarter, die again to a flanker I should've heard coming except my game audio is competing with the ambient hum of the mini-fridge and the occasional clank from the pipes in the wall.

<<eli "Okay, that was   yeah. Rough start. Let me try a different angle here.">>

[[Let me try a different angle|day_1_3]]

:: day_1_3
<<set $viewers to 5>>
<<set $day_step to 3>>
Five. A surge. Two entire new people decided to spend a fraction of their finite existence watching me get destroyed in a video game. I should be grateful. I should be performing.

<<eli "Alright chat, we're running it back. Silver lobbies, baby. The grind never stops.">>

A message appears in chat.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "ur still silver? lmaooo">>

There he is. My most loyal viewer. The only person on earth who consistently shows up to watch me stream, and he does it exclusively to be an asshole. I don't know if this counts as a parasocial relationship, but it's the closest thing I have to a fanbase.

<<eli "DarkSlayer, my man. Consistent as always.">>

I try to make it sound like banter. It doesn't.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "bro just quit already no one's watching">>

<<chat "BotStream_Promo" "🔥 Want MORE viewers? Visit StreamBoostPro.com for REAL growth! 🔥">>

And there's the other half of my audience. Bots. The chat feels like a waiting room in purgatory   one real person who hates me, and automated spam.

I push through the match. Manage to clutch a 1v2 that would be impressive if anyone were watching, if I were recording it, if the webcam quality could actually capture the play at a resolution higher than "vaguely humanoid shape moves mouse." Nobody clipped it. Nobody reacted. The kill feed updated and the round ended and I sat there for a half-second with that jolt of adrenaline that had nowhere to go   the high of a play with no audience, like screaming into a jar.

<<eli "Let's goooo! That was clean, chat. You see that? 1v2 clutch.">>

[[Someone left|day_1_4]]

:: day_1_4
<<set $viewers to 4>>
<<set $day_step to 4>>
Someone left. Someone watched me do the best thing I've done all week and their response was to navigate away from my stream.

The next twenty minutes blur together. I play, I die, I play again. My commentary gets thinner as the silence stretches. 

The thing about streaming to no one is that you can hear your own voice bouncing off the walls of the room, and after a while you start to realize that you're essentially talking to yourself in a room you're paying rent on, into a microphone nobody's listening to, while a camera nobody's watching records your face for no one to see. 

It's not sad. It's past sad. It's the thing that comes after sad, the flat grey territory where emotions can't even muster the energy to register anymore.

[[Check the chat|day_1_5]]

:: day_1_5
<<set $viewers to 6>>
<<set $day_step to 5>>
A spike. I sit up slightly.

<<chat "gg_riley" "first time here, what rank r u?">>

A new person. An actual new person asking an actual question.

<<eli "Hey, Riley! Welcome in, man. I'm Silver 2 right now, grinding toward Gold. Stick around, we're about to pop off.">>

<<chat "gg_riley" "oh silver, cool. gl">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "hes been silver for 3 months lol">>

Thanks, DarkSlayer. Really rounding out the new viewer experience.

gg_riley doesn't respond again. I check thirty seconds later   the viewer count hasn't dropped, but I know that doesn't mean anything. People leave tabs open. People fall asleep. People click on a stream by accident and leave it running while they take a shit.

I lose the next match. Badly. The kind of match where the other team is so much better that the scoreboard feels like a personal insult   2 kills, 13 deaths, last place on my team by a margin that suggests I might've contributed more by disconnecting and giving them a bot replacement.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "LMAO 2 and 13 just uninstall">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "actually uninstall streaming too">>

<<chat "BotStream_Promo" "🔥 TIRED of low viewers? StreamBoostPro.com   guaranteed growth! 🔥">>

I stare at the chat. At DarkSlayer's messages. At the viewer count   back to 4. At the donation ticker on my dashboard showing $0.00, which is the same number it showed yesterday and the day before and every day this week and every day last week.

My hands are on the keyboard but I'm not playing. The next match is loading and I'm not seeing it. I'm seeing the number. $0.00. The same number as my bank balance after rent. The same number as my contribution to anything.

I pick at the skin around my thumbnail. It's already raw from yesterday.

<<eli "Alright, you know what, I think   I think we'll do one more and then take a break. Stream's been going about an hour. Thanks for hanging out, everybody.">>

[[Take a break|day_1_5.1]]

:: day_1_5.1
<<set $viewers to 4>>
<<set $day_step to 5.1>>
Everybody. Four viewers. One troll, two bots, and someone who might already be asleep.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "'everybody' lol">>

I play the last match on autopilot. My crosshair moves, my fingers press keys, but I'm somewhere else   that hollow space behind the screen where you exist when you're performing for no one. I go 5 and 8. Mid. Unremarkable. The scoreboard is a biography.

<<eli "Alright, that's the stream. Thanks for watching, DarkSlayer. Seriously. At least you're consistent. I'll be back later tonight for another session. We'll get that Gold rank. Believe.">>

I hit "End Stream." The webcam light goes off. My face disappears from the preview. I sit in the silence.

The mini-fridge hums. The pipes clank. Somewhere outside, a car alarm starts and stops. The apartment is exactly as small as it was an hour ago, but it feels smaller now, like the walls shifted two inches inward while I was streaming. I check my dashboard   final stats for the session:

Peak Viewers: 6
Average Viewers: 4.2
New Followers: 0
Donations: $0.00
Subscriber Count: 0
Chat Messages: 7 (3 human, 4 bot)

I stare at those numbers until they stop being numbers and become a verdict. My phone buzzes on the desk. A text from Marcus.

<<marc "yo u alive">>

<<marc "come thru if u want. got the new DLC for Rift Walkers">>

I look at the numbers one more time. Then I close the dashboard, close OBS, close the game. I grab my hoodie   the dark navy zip-up that's more lint than fabric at this point, the one with the fraying cuffs and a small hole in the left pocket that I keep losing quarters through   and I put on the slip-on sneakers with the crushed heels and I leave.
<<set $hint to "Go to Marcus's apartment.">>
[[Go to Marcus's Apartment|neighbourhood]]


:: day_1_6
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 6>>
<<set $hint to "Hang out with Marc and play some games.">>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/neighbourhood.jpeg">>
<<set $locationName to "Neighborhood">>
<</nobr>>
The walk to Marcus's place is twelve minutes if I cut through the parking lot behind the laundromat and eight if I also jaywalk across building. I do both because I've never once in my life chosen the longer path to anywhere. The January air hits my face like a slap   dry, brittle cold that gets into your sinuses and sits there. I didn't bring a scarf because I don't own one. The hoodie does its best, which isn't much. I shove my hands in the pockets, feel the quarter-sized hole in the left one, and keep walking.

Halfway there, my phone buzzes again. Not Marc this time.

<<kira "look what a viewer sent me today omgggg">>

<img src="images/scenes/day_1/day_1_1.jpeg" alt="Figurine of Kira's Stream Avatar" class="location-image">

A photo follows. It's a custom figurine of her stream avatar   chibi-style, holding a tiny pixelated sword, with KiraByte written on the base in pink lettering. It's clearly handmade, probably 3D printed and hand-painted, and it's genuinely impressive. Somebody spent real hours making this for a girl they've never met, and looking at it gives me a feeling I can't quite categorize   somewhere between admiration and a low, quiet ache.

<<eli "that's actually sick. the detail on the sword is crazy">>

<<kira "RIGHT?? i literally almost cried on stream lol. some people are so sweet">>

<<kira "when you blow up i'll commission one of you too 😤">>

<<eli "bold of you to assume i'll blow up">>

<<kira "bold of you to assume you won't. i believe in you eli ❤️">>

I stop walking. Just for a second. Right there on the sidewalk in the January cold with my hands in my pockets and my crushed sneakers on the cracked concrete. She said she believes in me. With a heart emoji. A red one. Not a pink one, not a sparkle, a red heart. I know   I know   that Kira throws hearts around like confetti, that her Discord is littered with them, that she once sent a heart to a viewer who told her their cat learned to open doors. It doesn't mean what I want it to mean.

I keep walking.

<<eli "thanks kira">>

That's what I send. "Thanks kira." Two words. No emoji. No exclamation mark. The conversational equivalent of a firm handshake when someone opens their arms for a hug. I'm already composing better responses in my head   "that means a lot coming from someone who actually knows what she's doing" or "careful, I might hold you to that" or literally anything with warmth in it   but my thumbs have already sent the message and the moment has passed and I am, as always, three minutes too slow for the version of myself I want to be.

<<kira "always 💕">>

I put the phone away and walk faster because the cold is getting into my bones and because I need to stop analyzing single-word messages from a girl who is my friend. My friend. The word tastes like off-brand cola   close to the real thing but flat, missing the thing that makes it worth wanting.

[[Enter the Marc's Apartment|day_1_7]]

:: day_1_7
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 7>>
<<set $locationImage to "images/locations/marc_apartment.jpeg">>
<<set $locationName to "Marc's Apartment">>
<</nobr>>
Marc's apartment is on the second floor of a building that's marginally less depressing than mine   same general vibe of "landlord spends the maintenance budget on nothing," but his unit is bigger, has an actual bedroom separated by an actual wall, and benefits from the fact that Marcus is a functional human being who owns things like dish soap and a mop. He works part-time at a phone repair shop called ScreenFixx (two x's, because the owner thinks that's edgy), and between that and some money his mom sends every other month, he manages to live with a dignity I find both inspiring and quietly infuriating.

I buzz his unit. The intercom crackles.

<<marc "Yo.">>

<<eli "It's me.">>

The door buzzes. I take the stairs because the elevator in this building makes a sound like a submarine in a World War II movie and I don't trust it with my life, which is saying something given how little I currently value it.

Marc's door is already open when I get to the second floor. He's standing in the frame in an oversized charcoal hoodie and basketball shorts   in January, because Marcus's relationship with temperature is adversarial   holding a bag of cheese puffs.

<<marc "Bro. You look like shit.">>

<<eli "Thanks.">>

<<marc "No, like, more than usual.">>

<<eli "I appreciate the specificity.">>

I walk past him into the apartment. It's warm. It smells like something that might be food   there's a pot on the stove with a lid on it. Marcus cooks. Not well, and not ambitiously, but he cooks. He makes rice and chicken with jarred curry sauce and considers it haute cuisine. Compared to my diet of microwave burritos and regret, he's basically Gordon Ramsay.

His living room setup is aggressively casual: a sagging brown couch he got from his cousin, a 42-inch TV balanced on a milk crate and a stack of old textbooks, a secondhand PS5, and one controller that works properly and one with a sticky R2 trigger that you have to slam to get it to register. I always get the sticky one. I've stopped complaining about it because at this point it's tradition.

<<eli "DLC any good?">>

I drop onto the couch. It swallows me. The cushions have achieved a level of compression that's less "furniture" and more "fabric-covered surrender."

<<marc "Haven't started it yet. Waited for you.">>

He tosses me the sticky controller and settles into the other end of the couch.

<<marc "Figured you needed the distraction.">>

<<eli "What makes you say that?">>

<<marc "You texted me at 4 AM last night asking if penguins have knees.">>

<<eli "They do, by the way. Hidden inside the body. The whole leg is ">>

<<marc "Eli.">>

<<eli "What?">>

He looks at me. Marcus has this way of looking at people   not aggressive, not even particularly intense, just direct in a way that feels almost alien in a world where everyone's staring at a screen. His brown eyes hold you and don't let go.

<<marc "How'd the stream go?">>

<<eli "Fine.">>

<<marc "How many viewers?">>

<<eli "It was fine, Marc.">>

<<marc "So, like, twelve?">>

<<eli "...peaked at six.">>

He nods. Doesn't say anything. Turns on the TV, navigates to Rift Walkers, launches the DLC. No speech. No pep talk. No "you should try this" or "have you thought about that." Just   next thing. That's Marcus. He has this gift for knowing when the kindest thing you can do for someone is refuse to make a big deal out of their suffering. He just glides past it like it's a pothole in the road. Noted, avoided, moving on.

[[Start playing Rift Walkers|day_1_8]]

:: day_1_8
<<set $day_step to 8>>
We play for two hours. Rift Walkers is a co-op roguelike   procedurally generated dungeons, permadeath, the kind of game that's brutal and chaotic and requires constant communication. It's also the only context in which Marcus and I talk openly, because there's something about screaming "BEHIND YOU, BEHIND YOU, LEFT SIDE" while a demon boss is ripping your character's spine out that strips away the layers of bullshit we normally hide behind.

<<eli "Heal me, heal me   Marc, I'm literally one hit ">>

<<marc "I'm out of charges, bro, just dodge!">>

<<eli "Dodge WHAT? He's got six arms!">>

<<marc "Dodge the arms!">>

<<eli "Oh, thank you, incredible tactical advice ">>

I die. My character ragdolls across the screen in a way that would be funny if I hadn't been grinding this run for forty-five minutes. Marcus lasts another eight seconds before the boss catches him with a chain attack that turns his health bar into a memory.

RUN FAILED   FLOOR 14

<<marc "Floor 14.">>

He says it like he's reading a medical diagnosis.

<<eli "Floor 14.">>

<<marc "Our personal best is 16.">>

<<eli "I'm aware.">>

<<marc "Just making sure you're keeping track of our record of mediocrity.">>

I laugh. It sneaks out before I can stop it   a real one, short and surprised, like a cough. Marcus grins without looking at me, which is his version of a standing ovation.

We run it again. Die on floor 11. Run it again. Die on floor 9 because Marcus accidentally walks into a trap and I laugh so hard I forget to dodge and we both get wiped in the same explosion. The apartment fills with the sound of us yelling at each other   the friendly kind, the kind that has rhythm and call-and-response and a warmth underneath it that neither of us would ever name.

It's the best I've felt in weeks.

Between runs, while the loading screen spirals, Marcus glances at me sideways.

<<marc "You talk to Kira today?">>

My thumb slips on the sticky trigger. I play it off as adjusting my grip.

<<eli "She sent me a photo of some figurine a viewer made her.">>

<<marc "That's not what I asked.">>

<<eli "...yes. I talked to Kira today.">>

<<marc "She know you're in love with her yet?">>

<<eli "I'm not in love with her.">>

<<marc "Bro.">>

<<eli "She's my friend.">>

<<marc "Right. Your friend who you check your phone for every eleven seconds. Your friend whose stream schedule you have memorized. Your friend whose laugh you described to me once as   what was it   'annoyingly perfect'?">>

<<eli "I said that drunk.">>

<<marc "You had one beer.">>

<<eli "I'm a lightweight.">>

<<marc "You're a disaster.">>

The loading screen ends. A new dungeon generates. I'm grateful for the excuse to stop talking.

<<marc "All I'm saying is ">>

<<eli "Can we not?">>

<<marc "You should tell her.">>

<<eli "Tell her what? 'Hey Kira, I know you have 8000 viewers and brand deals and you're literally the most put-together person I've ever met, but I   a guy who peaked at six viewers today and ate a microwave burrito for breakfast   would like to ruin our friendship by making it weird.' Great plan, Marc.">>

Marcus is quiet for a beat. His character runs through a doorway on screen. Mine follows.

<<marc "You know she likes you, right?">>

<<eli "She likes everybody. That's her thing.">>

<<marc "Nah. She likes you you. The way she talks about you is different.">>

<<eli "You've never even met her.">>

<<marc "I've heard enough. You show me her messages like they're evidence in a court case, bro. I've been briefed.">>

<<eli "I don't   I don't show you her messages.">>

<<marc "You literally read me one out loud last Tuesday.">>

I open my mouth. Close it. He's right. She'd sent something about a game she wanted to play together   "we should do a collab stream sometime, i bet our vibes would be adorable together 🥺"   and I'd read it to Marcus while we were eating pizza, trying to sound casual and failing so comprehensively that he put his slice down and stared at me for a full five seconds without blinking.

<<eli "That was... I was asking for a second opinion on whether 'adorable' was flirty or friendly.">>

<<marc "And my opinion was flirty. And you ignored it. Like you ignore everything that doesn't confirm your theory that nobody could ever like you.">>

A skeleton archer shoots my character in the face. I die.

<<eli "Thanks for the therapy session. Can we play the game?">>

<<marc "I can do both.">>

<<eli "Please don't.">>

He lets it go. But I can feel him not letting it go   the way he glances at me during loading screens, the way the corner of his mouth twitches like he's biting back twelve more sentences. Marcus has this theory about me and Kira that he's constructed entirely from text messages I've shown him and stories I've told him and the way my voice apparently changes when I say her name (which it doesn't, he's making that up, it absolutely doesn't).

The theory is wrong. Not because Kira isn't incredible   she is. She's five-foot-three with a sharp bob and blunt bangs and wire-frame glasses that make her look like a character from a movie where the quiet girl turns out to be the main character all along. She's got this laugh   this high, sudden, slightly embarrassed laugh that she tries to suppress by pressing her lips together and it never works, it always breaks through, and it makes her whole face scrunch up in a way that 

The theory is wrong because even if Kira did like me, what would she be liking? This? A guy who can't afford coffee? A streamer with no stream? A face that doesn't stop anyone's scroll? The math doesn't work. Kira is a person who exists in the world with confidence and direction and a pixel-heart tattoo on her wrist that she got because she wanted to and could afford to and it suited her, and I am a person who exists in a studio apartment with a crack in the ceiling and $47 in the bank and a collection of character flaws that would embarrass a first draft.

We play until 3:30. My phone buzzes once more during the session   a meme from Kira, some screenshot of a cat sitting at a gaming desk with the caption "me at 3am grinding ranked"   and Marcus sees me smile at it and raises one eyebrow and I throw a cheese puff at his head and he catches it in his mouth and neither of us says anything.

<img src="images/scenes/day_1/day_1_2.jpeg" alt="Figurine of Kira's Stream Avatar" class="location-image">

Around 3:30, Marcus stands up, stretches   his spine cracks in a way that sounds medically concerning   and announces that he's hungry and we should go get coffee.

<<eli "I'm good.">>

I say it automatically.

<<marc "I didn't ask if you were good. I said we should go get coffee.">>

<<eli "I'm not really ">>

<<marc "Eli. We're getting coffee. Put your shoes on.">>

I put my shoes on.
<<set $hint to "Go to the coffee shop.">>
[[Go to the coffee shop|neighbourhood]]

:: day_1_9
<<set $day_step to 9>>
The coffee shop is called Grounded because every coffee shop opened in the last ten years is legally required to have a pun name. It's four blocks from Marcus's place, wedged between a dry cleaner and a nail salon, and it has the standard-issue indie café aesthetic: exposed brick, Edison bulbs, chalkboard menu, a barista with a septum piercing and an apron that says "Espresso Yourself." I've been here enough times to know the Wi-Fi password (grounded2024) and to know that their cheapest drip coffee is $4.75, which is roughly 38% of what I earned from streaming last month.

We stand in line. I look at the menu board with the detached interest of someone browsing a car dealership when they can't afford the bus.

<<marc "What do you want?">>

He's already pulling out his card.

<<eli "I'm fine, I ate before I came over.">>

<<marc "You ate a microwave burrito and a Monster.">>

<<eli "How do you ">>

<<marc "Because that's what you always eat. What do you want?">>

<<eli "Marc, I can't ">>

<<marc "Bro. Coffee. Not a kidney. What do you want?">>

I look at the menu. Something in my chest tightens. It's a small thing   a cup of coffee, four dollars and change   but the fact that I can't casually buy one without doing mental math against my rent and my power bill and the $47 left in my checking account makes it feel enormous. Like the coffee isn't a drink but a diagnosis. You are a person who cannot buy coffee.

<<eli "Medium drip. Black.">>

Marcus orders for both of us. He gets a caramel oat milk something that costs eight dollars and makes no apology for it. He pays by tapping his card against the reader with the ease of someone who doesn't flinch at transactions. I watch the card tap. I think about $0.00 on my stream dashboard. I think about $47 in my checking account. I think about the fact that Marcus paid for my coffee without hesitation and without drama and that this kindness, this small casual kindness, feels like the saddest thing that's happened to me all day.

We sit at a corner table. The coffee is good. I wrap both hands around the cup and let the heat soak into my palms. Marcus sits across from me, long legs stretched out under the table, scrolling through his phone with one hand and sipping his caramel nonsense with the other.

<<marc "You see the new Gunmetal patch notes?">>

<<eli "Yeah. They nerfed the Viper again.">>

<<marc "Good. That gun was bullshit.">>

<<eli "I was using that gun.">>

<<marc "Bro, everybody was using that gun. That's why it was bullshit.">>

<<eli "Well, now I'm going to be even worse.">>

<<marc "You were already bad.">>

<<eli "Thanks, Marc.">>

He grins.

<<marc "I'm just saying. Maybe switch to the shotgun. You can't miss with a shotgun.">>

<<eli "You wildly overestimate my abilities.">>

We talk about the game. We talk about a movie Marcus watched last night   some sci-fi thing he describes as "pretty mid but the explosions were fire." We talk about his coworker at ScreenFixx who got fired for dropping a customer's phone in the toilet and trying to blame it on the customer. Normal stuff. Easy stuff. The kind of conversation that doesn't ask anything of you and doesn't judge the answers you give.

Then, because Marcus cannot leave a wound alone:

<<marc "So Kira. She stream today?">>

<<eli "I don't know. Why?">>

<<marc "Just curious. She's usually on by now, right? Noon to four?">>

<<eli "I don't track her schedule.">>

Marcus looks at me over his caramel oat milk whatever. Just looks at me. He doesn't say anything. He doesn't have to. We both know I have her stream schedule memorized down to the half-hour. We both know I usually have her stream open in a background tab during my own streams, her chat scrolling on my second monitor like a window into a life that actually works. We both know this, and the silence between us is loud with it.

<<eli "...she usually goes live at twelve-thirty on weekdays.">>

<<marc "Mhm.">>

<<eli "That's public information. It's in her channel bio.">>

<<marc "Mhm.">>

<<eli "Stop mhm-ing at me.">>

<<marc "When are you seeing her next?">>

<<eli "We're not   it's not like ">>

I take a breath. Outside, a woman in a red coat walks past the window. Two girls my age laugh about something on a phone screen. The world moves and I sit here with my free coffee and my memorized knowledge of a girl's stream schedule and my complete inability to act on anything.

<<eli "She invited me to ramen tomorrow.">>

Marcus puts his cup down.

<<marc "Like a date?">>

<<eli "Like ramen. As friends. People eat ramen as friends, Marc.">>

<<marc "Did she say 'as friends'?">>

<<eli "She said she wanted to hang out.">>

<<marc "She say anything else?">>

<<eli "She said she'd pick me up.">>

Marcus makes a sound. It's not quite a laugh and not quite a word   some hybrid of the two that communicates an entire worldview.

<<marc "She's picking you up. For ramen.">>

<<eli "It's not a date.">>

<<marc "She's literally coming to your house to take you to a restaurant.">>

<<eli "It's not   we're friends. She does stuff like this. She's nice.">>

<<marc "She's nice to everybody. She's picking you up.">>

I drink my coffee. It's gone lukewarm but I don't care. My face feels warm in a way that has nothing to do with the drink and everything to do with the fact that Marcus has, as usual, found the exact thought I've been trying to bury and dragged it into the open air where it can breathe and embarrass me.

<<eli "Can we change the subject?">>

<<marc "Wear the grey shirt tomorrow. Less depressing.">>

<<eli "Marc.">>

<<marc "And wash your hair. Like actually wash it. With soap.">>

<<eli "I hate you.">>

<<marc "Nah. You love me. I'm the only person who tells you the truth.">>

He sips his drink. I look out the window. The January light is failing, that grey-blue winter afternoon that bleeds into dusk without warning. People walk by with their collars up and their faces down. I watch them and I feel that thing I always feel in public. That I'm watching the world from behind glass. That there's a performance of being alive happening out there and I never learned the choreography.

But underneath that   underneath the usual static of inadequacy   there's something else today. A small, warm, stupid thing. Tomorrow. Ramen. Kira coming to pick me up. Her car smells like that Japanese cherry blossom air freshener she clips to the vent. I've been in her car twice and I remember the scent perfectly, which is either observational skill or pathology.

<<marc "You streaming again tonight?">>

He doesn't look up from his phone.

<<eli "Probably.">>

<<marc "How's that going? Like, in general.">>

<<eli "It's going.">>

He nods. Doesn't push it. Sips his drink. I love and hate that about him   the way he asks questions with an escape hatch built in, giving me permission to dodge without making me feel bad for dodging. It's generous. It's also the reason I've never had to actually confront the answer.

How's it going?

It's not.

It's not going anywhere. It's been eight months. Fifty-four followers, most of whom followed during a brief spike when I got raided by a streamer who felt sorry for me and never came back. Eight to fifteen average viewers. Zero consistent income. I'm pouring hours into a void and the void isn't even polite enough to echo.

But I don't say that. I say "it's going" and I drink my coffee and Marcus lets me, because that's what we do.
<<set $hint to "Go back home. Start Stream">>
[[Go back to Home|neighbourhood]]

:: day_1_10
<<set $day_step to 10>>
I get home at 5:20 PM. The apartment is dark. I flip the light switch   the overhead flickers twice, because the bulb is dying and I haven't replaced it   and everything is exactly as I left it. Mattress on the floor. 

Desk with two monitors. Mini-fridge humming its one note. The microwave burrito wrapper on the counter that I didn't throw away this morning. The Monster can beside it, empty.

I take off the hoodie. Hang it on the back of the desk chair, which is where it lives. Kick off the crushed sneakers. Sit down.

The desk chair sinks an inch and a half under my weight, the hydraulic cylinder wheezing like an old man sitting down in church. I jiggle the mouse. 

Both monitors wake up   the main screen still showing Gunmetal Requiem's main menu, the second showing my stream dashboard with the final stats from this morning's session still displayed.
<<set $hint to "Check the stream dashboard.">>
[[Open OBS and check the dashboard|day_1_11]]

:: day_1_11
<<set $day_step to 11>>
Peak Viewers: 6
Average Viewers: 4.2
New Followers: 0
Donations: $0.00

I should eat something. Something real, something that wasn't manufactured in a factory and flash-frozen into a cylinder of regret. But cooking requires ingredients, which requires a grocery run, which requires money, and the $47 in my checking account has to last until... I don't actually know. Until the next donation, which could be never. Until I figure something out. Until the crack in the ceiling reaches the wall and the whole building collapses and I don't have to worry about rent anymore.

I eat another microwave burrito. Rotate it manually at the one-minute mark. This one is chicken and cheese, theoretically. It tastes like warm salt and texture.

At 7:00 PM I open OBS again.

This is the second stream of the day, which is a thing I do sometimes when the first one goes badly. The logic, if you can call it logic, is that evening hours have more viewers on Twitch overall, so maybe the browse page algorithm will push me to someone who's scrolling. It has never worked. But doing it feels like effort, and effort feels like it should matter, and that illusion is the only thing keeping me in this chair.
<<set $hint to "Stream">>
[[Start the evening stream|day_1_12]]

:: day_1_12
<<set $day_step to 12>>
<<set $viewers to 2>>
<<set $hint to "Play some Gunmetal Requiem">>
EliPlays   Live
Viewers: 2

Two. Less than this morning. I'm going backwards.

<<eli "Hey, welcome back, everyone. Evening stream   late night Gunmetal grind. Let's get it.">>

I queue into a match. The loading screen rotates. I adjust my mic, lean toward it, clear my throat.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "omg hes back for more">>

DarkSlayer. Of course. The man is a constant. If I ever go missing, the police should interview DarkSlayer first because he is the only person on earth who reliably knows my schedule.

<<eli "DarkSlayer, you're here twice in one day? I'm flattered. Truly.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "got nothing better to do">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "plus ur funny when u lose">>

<<eli "Glad my suffering entertains you.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "it does :)">>

The match starts. I'm playing Sentinel   defensive class, gadgets and traps, the kind of character that rewards smart positioning and punishes the kind of brainless aggression that is, unfortunately, my default instinct. I've been trying to improve. I watch YouTube guides. I practice in the range. I do the things you're supposed to do. And then a round starts and someone shoots at me and I panic-peek and die like I've never held a mouse before.

Round 1: I set up a trap on B-site, anchor position, play patient   and get wallbanged through a surface I didn't know was penetrable. Dead in four seconds.

<<eli "Cool. Okay. Didn't know you could shoot through that.">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "how do u not know that lmao">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "its been in the game since launch">>

[[Continue playing|day_1_13]]

:: day_1_13
<<set $day_step to 13>>
<<set $viewers to 3>>
Viewers: 3

Round 2: I play more passive. Sit in a corner with my crosshair aimed at the door. Nobody comes to my site. The action is all on A. My team loses 4v3 on the other side while I sit in silence staring at an empty hallway. I contributed nothing.

<<eli "Okay, they're pushing A every round. I'll rotate ">>

Round 3: I rotate to A. They push B. I'm always where the fight isn't.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "bro u have no game sense">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "like actually how are u this bad after 8 months">>

<<chat "newGuy_42" "is this guy actually trying or is this a bit">>

A new viewer. And that's their first message. That's the first impression   "is this guy actually trying."

<<eli "Haha, yeah, no, I'm   I'm trying. Just having an off game. Stick around, it'll get better.">>

<<chat "newGuy_42" "...ok">>

It does not get better. I finish the match 3-11. Bottom of the board by a mile. The loss screen lingers for a moment too long, showing everyone's stats in a column, and mine are at the bottom like a punchline.

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "3 and 11 KEKW">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "bro maybe try a different game">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "or a different hobby">>

<<chat "xX_DarkSlayer_Xx" "or a different life lol">>

[[Different life|day_1_14]]

:: day_1_14
<<set $day_step to 14>>
<<set $viewers to 2>>
newGuy_42 has left the channel.

I don't say anything for a second. My hands are on the keyboard. The match is loading. The webcam is on and my face is in the little preview window in OBS and I can see myself   the dark circles, the bad lighting, the greasy hair, the expression that's trying to be neutral and landing somewhere around "man contemplating whether his apartment's window opens far enough." I look small. In the preview, in the webcam's bad resolution, I look like a small grey thing in a dark room, hunched toward a screen, talking into a microphone to a crowd that fits in a sedan.

I play two more matches. Go 5-9 in one and 4-10 in the other. DarkSlayer narrates my failures with the enthusiasm of a sports commentator. Nobody else talks. The viewer count oscillates between 2 and 4 like a dying heartbeat on a monitor.

At 8:15 PM, I end the stream.

Session Stats:
Duration: 1 hour 15 minutes
Peak Viewers: 3
Average Viewers: 2.8
New Followers: 0
Donations: $0.00
Chat Messages: 14 (9 from DarkSlayer, 2 from newGuy_42, 3 from bots)

Zero dollars. Zero new followers. Zero reason to have done this.

I close OBS. Close the game. The main monitor shows my desktop. The second monitor shows the dashboard. I stare at both of them, at the dual display of my professional failure, and I feel a vibration in my pocket.

[[Check the phone|day_1_14.1]]

:: day_1_14.1
My phone. I pull it out. A Discord notification.

<<kira "heyyy eli! you free tomorrow? i'm taking a day off from streaming, thought we could hang out! there's this new ramen place near the art district that's supposed to be amazing 🍜">>

She already asked me this earlier today. Then I realize   she sent the ramen invite separately, after the figurine conversation. Like she planned it. Like she was working up to it and the figurine was the warm-up. Or maybe I'm reading into things because I'm a desperate person with $47 in the bank and a ceiling crack and a girl with blunt bangs is the only light source in my life.

I type back.

<<eli "yeah im free. ramen sounds good">>

<<kira "yay!! i'll send you the address. how was your stream today?">>

I look at the dashboard. $0.00.

<<eli "it was fine. pretty chill">>

<<kira "lol that's your answer every time. how many viewers?">>

<<eli "like... a few">>

<<kira "eli">>

<<eli "peaked at 6 this morning">>

<<kira "ok that's honestly not bad for your category and time slot! gunmetal is SUPER saturated rn">>

She does this   frames my failure as a strategic problem rather than a personal one. Like the issue is category selection, not me. It's kind. It's also inaccurate. The issue is absolutely me.

<<kira "have you thought about switching games? or doing just chatting?">>

<<eli "just chatting requires a personality people want to listen to">>

<<kira "you have a personality!!">>

<<eli "my most active viewer comes exclusively to insult me">>

<<kira "lmaooo darkslayer?">>

<<eli "the one and only">>

<<kira "ok but tbh that IS engagement. some streamers would kill for a regular. even a mean one 😂">>

<<eli "im not sure 'guy who watches me to say i suck' counts as a fan">>

<<kira "hey at least someone keeps coming back right? that's more than most people get starting out">>

<<kira "also... i've been thinking">>

Three dots appear. Disappear. Appear again. My heart does something structurally unsound.

<<kira "i know you don't like hearing this but you're actually funny. like genuinely. the dry self-deprecating thing works. if people could see that side of you more on stream you'd grow way faster">>

<<eli "you think my depression is marketable?">>

<<kira "LMAO stop 😭 i'm being serious!!">>

<<kira "like when we talk in discord you're so quick and witty and real. on stream you get all stiff and tryhardy. if you could just... be yourself?">>

<<eli "being myself is the problem kira">>

<<kira "no. being yourself is the solution. you just don't see it yet.">>

I stare at the screen. The cursor blinks in the text box. I type something. Delete it. Type something else. Delete that too. What I want to say is: you're the only person who makes me feel like there's a version of myself worth being. What I want to say is: when you talk about me like that I can almost believe it. What I want to say is a lot of things that live in the space between my ribs and have no business existing in a Discord DM.

<<eli "you're too nice to me">>

<<kira "i'm exactly the right amount of nice to you 😌">>

<<kira "ok but seriously. tomorrow. ramen. noon. i'm picking you up and you're not allowed to say no">>

<<eli "you already said that earlier">>

<<kira "i know. i'm saying it again so you don't chicken out overnight">>

<<eli "i wasn't going to chicken out">>

<<kira "mmhm. set an alarm. a real one. not the one you snooze for 90 minutes">>

<<eli "how do you know i snooze my alarm">>

<<kira "because i know you eli 😊">>

There it is again. That thing she does. I know you. Said with an emoji that could mean friendship or could mean something with more gravity, something that pulls, and I'll never ask which because asking would break the spell and I would rather live inside the ambiguity forever than know for certain that it's nothing.

<<eli "fine. alarm set. noon.">>

<<kira "good boy 😏">>

I almost drop my phone.

Good boy. She said good boy. With a smirk emoji. That's   that's flirty. That's objectively, measurably flirty. There's no friendship reading of "good boy" with a smirk emoji. Marcus would have a field day with this. Marcus would print this message on a t-shirt.

My thumbs hover. My face is hot. The apartment is cold and my face is hot and I am a 22-year-old man whose brain has been reduced to white noise by two words and a sideways smiley face.

<<eli "lol">>

I send "lol." I send the three laziest letters in the English language in response to the most charged two words anyone has ever aimed at me. I am a coward. I am a disaster. Marcus is right about everything.

<<kira "hehe. anyway i should sleep. early morning yoga tomorrow before i come get you">>

<<kira "night eli! don't stay up too late okay? you need your beauty sleep 😴✨">>

Beauty sleep. The same message from this morning. The same words I reread three times before getting out of bed. The cycle completes.

<<eli "night kira">>

I almost add a heart emoji. My thumb hovers over it   the red one, the one she uses, the one that means something or means nothing depending on who's pressing it. I don't add it. I send the message bare. Two words. Naked and small.

<<kira "💕">>

She sends two hearts anyway. Covers for both of us.

I put the phone down. Stare at the screen for a moment. She's having a good stream month   she mentioned it in passing last week, something about breaking her donation record. I didn't ask for specifics because I didn't want to know the number. Whatever it is, it would make my $12.50 look like what it is: not a career. Not even a hobby. A symptom.

I should do something productive. Work on my overlay. Edit a highlight reel. Research stream titles. Do any of the hundred things that content creators are supposed to do in their off-stream hours to build and maintain an audience.

I open YouTube. I watch three videos   one about stream growth tips (I've seen it before, it says the same things: be consistent, engage with chat, find your niche), one about the Gunmetal patch notes (the Viper nerf is worse than I thought), and one compilation of streamer clips where women with facecams and ring lights and perfect setups pull in thousands of viewers doing exactly what I do, except they're beautiful and I'm... me.

The last video is what gets me. Not the content   the comments. Hundreds of them.

<<chat "yt_comment_1" "She's so hot omg">>

<<chat "yt_comment_2" "I watch on mute just for the cam LOL">>

<<chat "yt_comment_3" "Those eyes could make me watch anything">>

<<chat "yt_comment_4" "Down bad for this streamer fr fr">>

I scroll through them. My thumb moves and the comments keep coming, an endless waterfall of thirst and admiration and money thrown at screens because the person behind the screen is pretty. I think about my webcam   the grainy rectangle, the bad lighting, the face that isn't ugly but isn't anything, the face that doesn't make anyone stop scrolling.

I think about Kira. How she once told me, offhandedly, that her donations tripled the day she started using a facecam. How she laughed about it, uncomfortable but pragmatic: "It's weird but it's the game, you know?" I think about her face   the sharp bob, the bangs, the glasses, the smirk   and how if she were in this compilation video, the comments would sound exactly the same. She's beautiful in the way that matters on the internet: specifically, photographically, streamably.

And then I think about "good boy" with a smirk emoji and my stomach does something that has no business happening in a body running on microwave burritos and Monster Energy.

I close YouTube. Close the browser. The apartment is silent. The fridge hums.
<<set $hint to "Go to bed.">>
[[Go to bed|apartment_map]]

:: day_1_15
<<nobr>>
<<set $day_step to 15>>
<<set $right_sidebar_mode to 0>>
<</nobr>>
I brush my teeth in the bathroom. I do not look in the mirror. I take off my joggers and get into bed   onto the mattress, onto the grey sheets, under the thin comforter that's not quite warm enough. I pull it up to my chin. The overhead light is off. The room is dark except for the faint glow of the second monitor's standby light and the green power LED on the router.

The ceiling crack is invisible in the dark but I know it's there. I stare at the space where it is and I think about the viewer count. About Marcus paying for my coffee. About DarkSlayer's messages and the bot spam and the four-dollar drink I couldn't afford and the $0.00 and the women on YouTube with their ring lights and their thousands of viewers and their comments full of money and desire and the fact that I'm 22 years old and the most consistent presence in my life is a stranger on the internet who shows up every day to tell me I suck.

I think about Kira. I think about her more than the rest of it. About the way she said "I know you" like it was a gift she was offering. About "good boy" and the smirk and the way my face went hot in a cold apartment. About her laugh   that high, sudden burst she can't suppress, the one that crinkles her nose and makes her push her glasses up because they slip when she scrunches her face. About the pixel-heart tattoo on her inner wrist that I've only seen in photos but want to see in person, up close, close enough to trace.

About the fact that she's picking me up tomorrow. That her car will smell like cherry blossom. That she'll probably be wearing something effortlessly perfect and I'll be in the grey t-shirt Marcus told me to wear and my black joggers and my crushed sneakers and she'll look at me and smile like none of that matters.

She makes me feel like none of it matters. That's the most dangerous thing about her.

I roll onto my side. The mattress creaks. The fitted sheet has popped off the corner again. My bare foot touches the stained part and I pull it back.

Tomorrow. Ramen with Kira. Her voice across a table. Her laugh. The chance that her knee might brush mine under it.

Something to look forward to. Something that isn't a stream, isn't a dashboard, isn't a number.

I close my eyes. The fridge hums. I sleep.

<<link "Sleep">>
<<set $hint to "Wake up the next day.">>
<<goto "eli_bed">>
<</link>>